---
title: GitLab Auth
description: Configure authentication for Nx Cloud using a GitLab app
filter: 'type:Guides'
---

Before creating your container, you'll need to create a GitLab OAuth application for your organization.

## Creating a GitLab app

Create the application in a GitLab group, not under your own profile.
GitLab deletes a personal application along with the account that owns it,
and Nx Cloud login stops working.
Pick a group that several people own, so the application stays when one of them leaves.

1. Open the group in GitLab.
1. Go to **Settings** > **Applications**.
1. Select **Add new application**.

You need the Owner role on the group to add an application.
On GitLab self-managed, an administrator can instead create an instance-wide application
under **Admin** > **Applications**.

Give the app a name. The authorization callback is the important bit. It needs to be in this form:

```text
[your-nx-cloud-url]/auth-callback

# for example
https://my.nx-enterprise.url:8080/auth-callback
```

**Important:** Ensure there is **no backslash at the end of the "Redirect URI"** (i.e. it matches the above pattern)

![GitLab group application form](../../../../assets/enterprise/single-tenant/gitlab-group-app-form.webp)

Ensure you grant it the "`read_user`" scope:

![GitLab application scopes with read_user selected](../../../../assets/enterprise/single-tenant/gitlab-group-app-scopes.webp)

Click "Save application".

Once you create, keep a note of the Client ID and the Secret:

![Created GitLab group application with its ID and secret](../../../../assets/enterprise/single-tenant/gitlab-group-app-created.webp)

## Connect your Nx Cloud installation to your new app

Contact your developer productivity engineer to connect your Nx Cloud instance to the newly created GitLab app.
